import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'1.1'))
from authoring_support import Topic
t=Topic('3.1')
t.claim('01','A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers interact to exchange a product or resource.','definition')
t.claim('01','Market interaction can occur through a physical meeting or through communication and payment arrangements connecting people at different locations.')
t.claim('01','Market participants communicate offers, requirements and terms, while exchange depends on an acceptable arrangement being reached.')
t.claim('02','Product markets exchange goods and services, whereas factor markets exchange productive inputs such as labour services.')
t.claim('02','In a labour market, workers supply labour services and employers demand them.')
t.claim('02','Markets can also be described by geographic reach or by how buyers and sellers communicate; these dimensions do not replace the product/factor distinction.')
t.block('01','definition','A market is an arrangement, not just a place',"""
A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers interact to exchange a product or resource. A physical stall is one possible setting, but a market does not require everyone to gather in the same square or building. Buyers and sellers can communicate by telephone, through messages or through an online ordering system. What matters economically is that they can identify what is being offered, communicate requirements and reach terms for an exchange.

Use the invented Namibian business Tsumeb Threadworks. A household sends a message asking to buy a bag, the seller quotes a price, and the parties arrange payment and collection. Their interaction forms part of a market even though the order was agreed remotely. An advertisement without any willing buyer does not by itself prove that an exchange has occurred; communication creates an opportunity to trade, while an acceptable agreement is still needed.
""")
t.block('02','comparison','Product markets and factor markets',"""
A product market exchanges goods or services for use by customers. The bag bought from Threadworks is a product. A repair service sold to a household also belongs in a product market even though the service is not a physical object. Identify what is being exchanged rather than assuming that a market must sell a tangible good.

A factor market exchanges productive inputs. If Threadworks hires a worker to stitch bags, it demands labour services, while the worker supplies those services in return for pay. The worker’s search for a job does not make the worker the demander of labour in this economic classification. The relevant input is the work supplied to production. The firm can be a seller in the bag market and a buyer in the labour market at the same time.

Classification depends on the transaction. Buying a finished bag and hiring someone’s working time are different exchanges, even when they involve the same business. State the product or input, name the buyer and seller, and explain what each provides. This method avoids deciding the market type merely from the identity of one participant.
""")
t.block('02','plain_explanation','Other ways to describe a market',"""
Geographic descriptions concern the reach of trading. A local market connects a more limited area; a national market reaches buyers and sellers across a country; an international market involves cross-border trade. The label needs evidence about the reach of the activity. A small seller can use remote communication to obtain customers outside its immediate town, so the size of its premises alone does not establish its market’s boundary.

Physical and remote arrangements describe how participants interact. They can exist alongside geographic and product classifications. An online sale can be a national product-market transaction; a locally agreed job can be a factor-market transaction. These descriptions answer different questions, so one does not cancel another.
""")
t.block('01 02','cross_link','From market arrangements to market forces',"""
Topic 1.2 explains market allocation as part of an economic system; this topic identifies the arrangement connecting participants. Topic 3.2 then explains demand, supply and equilibrium within a market. Topic 4.2 applies related reasoning to earnings. Establish the traded item and participant roles first, so that later explanations of price or pay refer to the correct market rather than treating every transaction as a purchase of a finished good.
""")
def s(sub):return next(x for x in t.wo['item_slots'] if x['subtype']==sub)
t.card(s('DEF'),'01','Define a market.','An arrangement through which buyers and sellers interact to exchange a product or resource.',['Meaning/definition and essential element (1): interaction of buyers and sellers for exchange, without requiring one physical location.'])
t.card(s('FEATURE'),'01','Identify two features of a market arrangement when a household orders a bag from an invented Namibian workshop by message.','A buyer and seller communicate about an exchange; they agree terms such as price, payment and delivery.',['Characteristic (1): buyers and sellers interact. Distinction/example (1): terms of product exchange are communicated/agreed.'])
t.card(s('DIST'),'02','Explain the difference between a product market and a labour market.','A product market exchanges goods or services; a labour market exchanges workers’ labour services as a productive input, with employers demanding them.',['Difference (1): finished goods/services versus productive labour input. Contrast/test (1): identify what is exchanged and who buys/supplies it.'])
t.card(s('APP'),'02','Explain the market classification when an invented workshop hires a worker to stitch bags rather than buying a finished bag.','It is a factor/labour-market transaction because the firm obtains labour services used in production, not a finished product.',['Context (1): hiring stitching labour. Reason/conclusion (1): productive input exchange establishes factor-market classification.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][0]
t.card(p,'01 02','Define market (2). Identify the market type for a household buying a finished bag and for a workshop hiring stitching labour (2). Explain how a message order can form part of a market without a physical meeting (2). Identify what is traded in each of those two transactions (2).','A market connects buyers and sellers for exchange. The finished bag is traded in a product market; hiring labour is a factor-market transaction. Messages communicate the offer and terms so agreement and payment can be arranged remotely. The traded items are a bag and labour services respectively.',['Meaning (2): buyers/sellers interact (1), for exchange of a product/resource (1). Separate points for types (2): product (1), factor/labour (1). Explanation (2): remote communication connects participants (1), terms/payment can be agreed without meeting (1). Traded items (2): finished bag (1), labour services (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][1]
t.card(p,'01 02','Invented Namibian case: a household orders a finished bag by message from Tsumeb Threadworks, agreeing price and collection. The workshop separately hires a worker for stitching time at agreed pay. Describe three features of the market arrangements (6). Explain the buyer/seller roles in the bag transaction and the possibility of remote exchange (4). Explain the roles in the labour transaction and its difference from buying a finished bag (4).','The transactions connect willing participants, concern a stated product or resource, and establish agreed terms. The household is the buyer of the bag and Threadworks the seller; messages communicate the offer and permit terms/payment arrangements without a physical meeting. In the labour transaction, the workshop demands working time and the worker supplies it for pay. This is an input used to produce goods rather than a household buying finished output.',['Context/data (6): participants interact (1), household/workshop and worker are stated (1); specified traded items (1), bag and stitching time (1); terms agreed (1), price/collection or pay given (1). Mechanism/explanation part 1 (4): household buys bag (1), workshop sells it (1), messages communicate terms (1), exchange can be arranged remotely (1). Explanation part 2 (4): firm demands labour (1), worker supplies it (1), pay rewards supplied work (1), input exchange differs from buying the finished bag (1). Marks allocation 6+4+4=14.'])
t.finish()
